1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,905
Here's a space-vector locus, like those in
Lesson 11,

2
00:00:03,945 --> 00:00:09,454
from a drive's two current sensors: a
synthetic log we'll take apart at the end.

3
00:00:09,494 --> 00:00:13,836
It should be a circle centred on the
origin. At first glance, it is.

4
00:00:13,936 --> 00:00:17,770
Magnify its deviation from a centred
circle five times, though,

5
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:21,999
and it's lopsided: stretched one way, and
pushed off-centre.

6
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:25,983
Is that the motor, or the sensors? Today:
the Clarke transformation,

7
00:00:26,023 --> 00:00:28,946
and how to read the answer from the shape.

8
00:00:30,246 --> 00:00:32,733
First, the answers from Lesson 11.

9
00:00:32,773 --> 00:00:37,548
One: balanced currents of 10 A peak give a
space vector of exactly 10 A.

10
00:00:37,588 --> 00:00:40,222
That's what the two-thirds is for.

11
00:00:40,262 --> 00:00:46,581
Two: adding 1 A of DC to all three phases
changes nothing. It's pure zero sequence.

12
00:00:46,621 --> 00:00:51,962
Three: with phase c open, the vector
slides along a line at −30°,

13
00:00:52,002 --> 00:00:58,296
reaching 11.5 A when phase a peaks at 10.
It pulsates; it never turns.

14
00:00:59,596 --> 00:01:02,497
Every complex number is two real numbers:

15
00:01:02,537 --> 00:01:07,559
the real part of the space vector is i_α,
the imaginary part i_β.

16
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:12,398
α lies along the axis of phase a; β is 90°
ahead.

17
00:01:12,438 --> 00:01:16,667
Project the three phase contributions onto
these axes:

18
00:01:16,707 --> 00:01:21,331
that's the Clarke transformation. i_α is
two-thirds of i_a,

19
00:01:21,371 --> 00:01:26,732
minus half i_b, minus half i_c; i_β is i_b
minus i_c, over √3.

20
00:01:26,772 --> 00:01:29,900
Balanced currents become two sinusoids in
quadrature,

21
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:32,492
each with the full phase amplitude.

22
00:01:32,532 --> 00:01:36,009
The name honours Edith Clarke of General
Electric,

23
00:01:36,049 --> 00:01:41,050
who introduced these α, β and zero
components in the late 1930s.

24
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:44,103
Here's a more geometric view.

25
00:01:44,143 --> 00:01:48,647
Three phase currents are one point in a
space with axes a, b and c.

26
00:01:48,687 --> 00:01:51,549
As balanced currents flow, the point
traces a circle

27
00:01:51,589 --> 00:01:54,840
in the plane where the three currents add
to zero.

28
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:59,800
Now look straight down the diagonal, where
all three currents are equal.

29
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:06,122
The three axes, at right angles in this
space, now appear 120° apart:

30
00:02:06,162 --> 00:02:10,725
the phase axes of Lesson 11, around the
α–β plane.

31
00:02:10,765 --> 00:02:14,297
A zero-sequence current moves the point
along the diagonal,

32
00:02:14,337 --> 00:02:17,683
straight toward us. From here, nothing
changes.

33
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:20,939
Write the projections as a matrix,

34
00:02:20,979 --> 00:02:25,038
and add a third row for the zero sequence
— so nothing is lost.

35
00:02:25,078 --> 00:02:29,954
Now there's an inverse. Going back, i_a is
i_α plus i_0,

36
00:02:29,994 --> 00:02:35,896
and i_b and i_c take minus half of α, plus
or minus √3/2 of β.

37
00:02:35,936 --> 00:02:40,485
The drive uses it on the way out: the
controller decides v_α and v_β;

38
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:43,885
the half-bridges need three phase
voltages.

39
00:02:45,185 --> 00:02:50,401
Replace two-thirds with √(2/3), and the
zero row's one-third with 1/√3:

40
00:02:50,441 --> 00:02:53,392
that's the power-invariant version.

41
00:02:53,432 --> 00:02:57,709
Its rows are orthonormal, so its inverse
is its transpose:

42
00:02:57,749 --> 00:03:01,133
a pure rotation of abc space. Lengths
survive,

43
00:03:01,173 --> 00:03:07,587
so the 10 A circle keeps its radius of
12.2; and so do dot products, like power.

44
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:13,053
The amplitude-invariant matrix is the same
rotation, shrunk by √(2/3) in α–β.

45
00:03:13,093 --> 00:03:18,778
Undo the shrink in a product of two
quantities, and out comes three-halves.

46
00:03:18,818 --> 00:03:20,895
This series keeps peak values instead,

47
00:03:20,935 --> 00:03:24,556
so its power and torque formulas carry the
three-halves.

48
00:03:25,856 --> 00:03:31,874
Most drives measure only two currents, and
compute the third as minus i_a, minus i_b.

49
00:03:31,914 --> 00:03:36,033
Then Clarke shrinks to two lines: i_α is
just i_a,

50
00:03:36,073 --> 00:03:39,445
and i_β is i_a plus 2 i_b, over √3.

51
00:03:39,485 --> 00:03:42,030
That quietly assumes the currents add to
zero,

52
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:46,027
which Kirchhoff guarantees in a star
connection without neutral.

53
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:50,316
If a zero-sequence current does flow, say
a third harmonic through a

54
00:03:50,356 --> 00:03:55,673
connected neutral, it reappears at twice
its size, along the negative c axis.

55
00:03:55,713 --> 00:03:57,884
It also assumes two perfect sensors,

56
00:03:57,924 --> 00:04:02,048
read at the same instant. Let's break
those, one at a time.

57
00:04:03,348 --> 00:04:07,460
First, gain. Say the phase-a sensor reads
5 % high.

58
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:11,920
The error is 5 % of i_a, and it always
lies on the same line:

59
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:16,296
30°, where channel a lands in the two-
sensor formula.

60
00:04:16,336 --> 00:04:20,543
Back and forth along a line, plus a
rotation,

61
00:04:20,583 --> 00:04:26,705
makes an ellipse: semi-axes of 10.54 and
9.96 A, tilted 15°.

62
00:04:26,745 --> 00:04:31,595
In Lesson 11's terms, a backward-turning
part appears:

63
00:04:31,635 --> 00:04:35,576
0.29 A, which is 5 % of 10 A, over √3.

64
00:04:36,876 --> 00:04:41,163
Next, offset. Give channel a 200 mA of DC.

65
00:04:41,203 --> 00:04:45,837
Every point moves by the same vector, so
the circle shifts:

66
00:04:45,877 --> 00:04:51,010
0.23 A at 30°, the zero-hertz line of
Lesson 11's spectrum.

67
00:04:51,050 --> 00:04:52,748
The centre maps back uniquely:

68
00:04:52,788 --> 00:04:56,551
inverse Clarke of the centre gives each
channel's offset.

69
00:04:56,591 --> 00:05:01,068
In a frame turning with the current,
Lesson 13, a fixed offset becomes a

70
00:05:01,108 --> 00:05:06,335
ripple at the electrical frequency; a gain
mismatch, a ripple at twice that.

71
00:05:06,375 --> 00:05:10,206
Close the current loop, and the controller
smooths the measured current,

72
00:05:10,246 --> 00:05:13,326
so the real current and torque carry the
ripple.

73
00:05:14,626 --> 00:05:19,869
Last, timing: say channel b lags by 3
electrical degrees,

74
00:05:19,909 --> 00:05:24,355
just 25 µs at 5000 rpm on our 8-pole
motor.

75
00:05:24,395 --> 00:05:31,555
Again an ellipse, but its long axis sits
at −30°: 45° away from the gain case.

76
00:05:31,595 --> 00:05:34,872
Multiply the backward part by the forward
part,

77
00:05:34,912 --> 00:05:40,424
cancelling the current's phase: a gain
error lands at 30°, a lag at −60°.

78
00:05:40,464 --> 00:05:43,848
At right angles, so one complex number
separates them.

79
00:05:43,888 --> 00:05:49,398
The cure is in hardware: sample both
channels at once, through matched filters.

80
00:05:50,698 --> 00:05:55,183
Here's Clarke as it runs in a drive, once
every PWM period.

81
00:05:55,223 --> 00:05:59,982
The constants are computed offline. Each
sample costs two multiplies and an

82
00:06:00,022 --> 00:06:05,281
add going in, two of each coming back: no
roots, no trigonometry, no loops.

83
00:06:05,321 --> 00:06:11,374
On a fixed-point chip, 1/√3 becomes 18,919
over 32,768,

84
00:06:11,414 --> 00:06:17,081
and the result stays within one count of
exact.

85
00:06:17,121 --> 00:06:20,455
Calibration comes first: subtract each
channel's offset,

86
00:06:20,495 --> 00:06:24,128
measured with the inverter off, then trim
the gains.

87
00:06:25,428 --> 00:06:31,069
Now the exercise, on a synthetic log: 10 A
at 100 Hz, sampled at 20 kHz,

88
00:06:31,109 --> 00:06:36,382
with channel a 5 % high, offsets on both
channels, and noise.

89
00:06:36,422 --> 00:06:41,814
Over whole cycles, its complex FFT is a
least-squares ellipse fit: the

90
00:06:41,854 --> 00:06:48,599
zero-hertz line is the centre; the ±100 Hz
lines, the forward and backward parts.

91
00:06:48,639 --> 00:06:55,627
The centre, through inverse Clarke, gives
offsets of +0.15 and −0.25 A. Solving

92
00:06:55,667 --> 00:07:03,421
the backward part for channel b's complex
gain gives a ratio of 1.050, and no lag.

93
00:07:03,461 --> 00:07:06,548
Correct the channels, and the locus is a
centred circle,

94
00:07:06,588 --> 00:07:09,337
with the backward line down in the noise.

95
00:07:09,377 --> 00:07:14,455
The locus gives only the gain ratio;
absolute gains need a reference, such as a

96
00:07:14,495 --> 00:07:20,373
calibrated probe. On your own drive, log
both raw channels and run the same script.

97
00:07:21,673 --> 00:07:23,953
Three predictions before next time.

98
00:07:23,993 --> 00:07:26,392
One: three sensors, one per phase,

99
00:07:26,432 --> 00:07:32,170
and the phase-a sensor reads 5 % high.
What does the α–β locus look like?

100
00:07:33,270 --> 00:07:37,984
Two: back to two sensors, with a 0.3 A
offset on channel b only.

101
00:07:38,024 --> 00:07:40,456
What happens to the locus?

102
00:07:41,556 --> 00:07:46,517
Three: in which frame would the circle of
balanced currents become a single point?

103
00:07:47,617 --> 00:07:51,104
Next time: the Park transformation —
rotating frames,

104
00:07:51,144 --> 00:07:56,568
the rotor angle from Hall sensors or an
encoder, and what angle errors do.
